"""AP Biology 원형 P — BI3/BI4 재배정(2026-10-19): 현재 공개 Biology 1·2 의 실제 문항·원형을 원격 읽기로 확인한 뒤에도 비는 4칸(단원6×P1 1, 단원7×P1 2, 단원7×P2 1)을 메우는 고정 문장 은행 원형."""
from bio_common import *  # noqa: F401,F403

bi_p_gene_regulation_concept = make_bank_archetype("bi_p_gene_regulation_concept", "6.3", "1.B", [
    B("In E. coli, the genes for lactose-digesting enzymes are transcribed at a high rate only when lactose is present and glucose is scarce. A student wants to explain why transcription of these genes increases when lactose is added to the medium. Which of the following best explains the effect of lactose?",
      ("Lactose, or a molecule made from it, binds to the repressor and changes its shape, so the repressor leaves the operator and RNA polymerase can transcribe the genes.", "Binding of the inducer to the repressor releases the operator and allows transcription."),
      [("Lactose binds to the promoter itself and increases the rate at which RNA polymerase moves along the DNA.", "The inducer acts on the repressor protein and not by binding the promoter."), ("Lactose is broken down to give the cell energy, and the added ATP turns on the genes.", "The regulation is by the shape of the repressor and not by an energy supply."), ("Lactose changes the DNA sequence of the operator so that the repressor protein can no longer bind to it.", "The operator sequence is not changed; the repressor protein changes shape.")]),
    B("In a eukaryotic cell, a transcription factor must bind to an enhancer region of the DNA before a gene is transcribed at a high rate. A mutation changes the shape of the DNA-binding domain of the transcription factor so that it cannot bind to the enhancer. Which of the following is the most likely effect of the mutation on the cell?",
      ("The gene is transcribed at a much lower rate, because the transcription factor cannot help to assemble the transcription machinery at the promoter.", "Transcription factors that bind enhancers increase the rate of transcription."),
      [("The gene is transcribed at a higher rate, because the enhancer is now free to bind RNA polymerase.", "The enhancer binds regulatory proteins and does not bind RNA polymerase directly."), ("The gene is transcribed at the same rate, because the promoter sequence has not changed.", "The promoter alone gives only a low rate of transcription without the activator."), ("The mRNA is transcribed but is not translated, because the transcription factor is needed by the ribosome.", "A transcription factor acts on transcription and not on translation.")]),
    B("Two cell types in the same person contain the same genes, but cell type X makes the protein hemoglobin and cell type Y does not. Which of the following best explains this difference?",
      ("The cell types express different regulatory proteins, so the hemoglobin gene is transcribed in X and not in Y.", "Differential gene expression results from differences in the regulatory proteins that act on the same DNA."),
      [("The hemoglobin gene has been deleted from the DNA of the cells of type Y.", "Cells of one individual carry the same genome."), ("The hemoglobin gene is present only in the nucleus of cell type X.", "Both cell types have the gene in their nuclei."), ("Cell type Y has no ribosomes at all, and so it cannot make any protein, including hemoglobin.", "Ribosomes are present in cells that make any protein; the difference is in which genes are transcribed.")]),
], "explain how a regulatory protein changes the transcription of a gene", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 6.3", est=65)

bi_p_selection_pattern = make_bank_archetype("bi_p_selection_pattern", "7.4", "1.B", [
    B("In a population of mice, individuals with very light fur and individuals with very dark fur are caught by predators more often than mice with medium-colored fur, which blend in with the soil. After many generations, which of the following is most likely to be observed in the population?",
      ("Most mice have medium-colored fur, and the range of fur colors is narrower than before, because stabilizing selection removes the extremes.", "When the intermediate phenotype has the highest fitness, the extremes become less common."),
      [("Most mice have very dark fur, because directional selection favors a single extreme.", "Both extremes are selected against and so the mean does not move toward one extreme."), ("Mice of very light and very dark fur become more common than mice of medium fur, because disruptive selection favors the extremes.", "The extremes have lower fitness here and are therefore selected against."), ("The mice all have the same fur color, because natural selection creates new alleles for the needed color.", "Selection acts on existing variation and does not create alleles to match a need.")]),
    B("A species of beetle lives in a forest in which the bark of trees has become darker over several decades because of soot. Dark beetles are eaten less often than light beetles. Which of the following best describes the expected change in the beetle population?",
      ("The mean color of the population shifts toward dark, because directional selection favors the dark phenotype.", "A shift toward one extreme occurs when that phenotype has the highest fitness."),
      [("The mean color remains the same, because individual beetles change their color to match the bark.", "Individual organisms do not change their genotype in response to the environment."), ("The population splits into light and dark groups with few medium beetles, because of disruptive selection.", "Only the dark phenotype has an advantage, so the whole population shifts."), ("Both colors become equally common, because predators eat each color equally.", "The dark beetles are eaten less, so they do not become equally common.")]),
    B("A population of finches lives on an island where seeds are either very small or very large. Birds with medium-sized beaks cannot handle either kind of seed efficiently, and birds with small or large beaks feed well. Which of the following is the most likely long-term change in the beak sizes of the population?",
      ("Birds with small beaks and birds with large beaks become more common than birds with medium beaks, because disruptive selection favors both extremes.", "When both extremes have higher fitness than the intermediate, the distribution becomes bimodal."),
      [("All birds eventually have medium-sized beaks, because stabilizing selection favors the average.", "The medium phenotype has the lowest fitness here."), ("All birds eventually have large beaks, because directional selection favors the largest size.", "Both small and large beaks are favored."), ("The beak sizes do not change, because the genes for beak size are fixed in each bird.", "The allele frequencies change in the population while the genotype of each bird does not.")]),
], "identify directional, stabilizing and disruptive selection from the fitness of phenotypes", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 7.4", est=65)

bi_p_reproductive_isolation = make_bank_archetype("bi_p_reproductive_isolation", "7.10", "1.B", [
    B("Two closely related species of frogs live in the same pond. One species breeds in early spring and the other species breeds in late summer. Which of the following best describes the type of reproductive isolation between the two species?",
      ("Prezygotic isolation, because the species breed at different times and so no zygotes are formed.", "A barrier that prevents mating or fertilization acts before the zygote forms."),
      [("Postzygotic isolation, because the hybrid offspring are not fertile.", "No hybrids form because the species never mate."), ("Postzygotic isolation, because the zygotes die early in development.", "No zygotes are formed at all in this example."), ("Geographic isolation, because the species live in different places.", "The two species live in the same pond, so this is not geographic isolation.")]),
    B("A horse and a donkey can mate and produce a mule. The mule is healthy but is sterile and cannot produce offspring. Which of the following best describes the type of reproductive isolation between horses and donkeys?",
      ("Postzygotic isolation, because the hybrid offspring are formed but are sterile.", "A barrier that acts after the zygote forms and reduces the fitness of hybrids is postzygotic."),
      [("Prezygotic isolation, because the two species can mate with each other.", "Mating occurs in this example, so the barrier is not in the mating behavior."), ("Prezygotic isolation, because the sperm and egg do not fuse.", "Fertilization does occur because a mule is produced."), ("No isolation, because the two species produce an offspring.", "The sterility of the mule keeps gene flow between the species from occurring.")]),
    B("Two species of plants that live in the same region cannot exchange pollen because one is pollinated only by bees and the other only by hummingbirds. Which of the following best describes the type of reproductive isolation?",
      ("Prezygotic isolation, because the different pollinators prevent pollen from being transferred between the species.", "The barrier prevents fertilization from happening, so it acts before the zygote."),
      [("Postzygotic isolation, because the seeds that form are not viable.", "No seeds form between the species in this example."), ("Postzygotic isolation, because the hybrid plants have a lower fitness.", "No hybrids form between the species in this example, so no hybrid fitness is involved."), ("Allopatric speciation, because the plants are separated by a physical barrier.", "The plants live in the same region and no geographic barrier is described.")]),
], "classify a reproductive barrier as prezygotic or postzygotic", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 7.10", est=60)

bi_p_allele_frequency_graph = make_bank_archetype("bi_p_allele_frequency_graph", "7.3", "2.A", [
    B("The graph shows the frequency of a green-color allele in a population of beetles measured at five times over 40 generations. Which of the following best describes the data in the graph?",
      ("The frequency of the green allele increased over time and then stayed nearly constant after generation 30.", "The bars rise from 0.10 to about 0.90 and the last two bars are nearly equal."),
      [("The frequency of the green allele decreased over time, as the allele was lost from the population.", "The bars rise from left to right and do not fall."),
       ("The frequency of the green allele stayed at about 0.10 in every generation that was measured.", "Only the first bar is about 0.10; the others are much higher."),
       ("The number of beetles in the population increased until generation 30 and then stayed constant.", "The vertical axis shows the allele frequency and not the population size.")],
      stim=bar_stim("Frequency of a green-color allele in a beetle population at five times", "Green allele frequency over 40 generations", ["0", "10", "20", "30", "40"], [("Allele frequency", [0.10, 0.35, 0.70, 0.88, 0.90])], "Generation", "Frequency of the green allele")),
    B("The graph shows the mean beak depth of a population of finches measured in the year before a drought and in the year after the drought. Which of the following best describes the data in the graph?",
      ("The mean beak depth was greater after the drought than before the drought.", "The bar for the year after the drought is taller than the bar for the year before."),
      [("The mean beak depth was smaller after the drought than before the drought.", "The bar after the drought has the greater height."),
       ("The mean beak depth was the same in the two years that were measured.", "The two bars have different heights."),
       ("Each bird's own beak grew larger during the drought, and so the mean rose.", "The graph shows the mean of the population and not the change in any one bird.")],
      stim=bar_stim("Mean beak depth of a finch population before and after a drought", "Mean beak depth before and after a drought", ["Year before drought", "Year after drought"], [("Mean beak depth (mm)", [9.2, 10.1])], "Time of measurement", "Mean beak depth (mm)")),
    B("The graph shows the percent of bacteria in a culture that are resistant to an antibiotic on four days of an experiment in which the antibiotic was present. Which of the following best describes the data in the graph?",
      ("The percent of resistant bacteria increased over the six days, with the largest increase between day 2 and day 4.", "The bars rise, and the step from day 2 to day 4 is the largest."),
      [("The percent of resistant bacteria decreased between day 2 and day 4 as the antibiotic killed them.", "The bar for day 4 is taller than the bar for day 2."),
       ("The percent of resistant bacteria increased by the same amount between each pair of days.", "The steps between the bars are not equal; the step from day 2 to day 4 is the largest."),
       ("The percent of bacteria that were killed by the antibiotic increased over the six days.", "The graph shows the percent that is resistant and not the percent that is killed.")],
      stim=bar_stim("Percent of bacteria that are resistant to an antibiotic on days 0, 2, 4 and 6", "Percent of resistant bacteria over six days", ["0", "2", "4", "6"], [("Resistant bacteria (%)", [1, 8, 55, 85])], "Time (days)", "Resistant bacteria (%)")),
], "describe the trend shown by a bar graph of allele frequency or a population trait", "fixed fact bank checked against CED 7.3", est=60)

